WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.556 --> 00:00:02.971
(speaking foreign language)
- Hear that?

00:00:02.971 --> 00:00:07.009
That is a military code
of the Choctaw nation.

00:00:07.009 --> 00:00:08.159
Can't understand it?

00:00:08.159 --> 00:00:10.126
Neither could the Germans.

00:00:10.126 --> 00:00:13.043
(orchestral music)

00:00:15.312 --> 00:00:17.771
Our story starts on the Western
front during Word War I.

00:00:17.771 --> 00:00:19.199
The Allies are in trouble.

00:00:19.199 --> 00:00:20.411
The Germans have tapped their phone lines

00:00:20.411 --> 00:00:22.261
and deciphered their codes.

00:00:22.261 --> 00:00:24.160
As the Allies struggled
to find a solution,

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:25.107
one captain overheard

00:00:25.107 --> 00:00:27.119
two of his Native
American Choctaw soldiers

00:00:27.119 --> 00:00:28.832
speaking their mother tongue.

00:00:28.832 --> 00:00:30.582
When the captain
overheard the two soldiers

00:00:30.582 --> 00:00:32.163
speaking their native language--

00:00:32.163 --> 00:00:33.181
- [Nuchi] They probably thought

00:00:33.181 --> 00:00:35.432
they were in really big trouble.

00:00:35.432 --> 00:00:37.133
- That's Nuchi Nashoba.
- Hi there.

00:00:37.133 --> 00:00:37.966
- [Narrator] Great-granddaughter of one

00:00:37.966 --> 00:00:38.799
of those two soldiers

00:00:38.799 --> 00:00:41.663
and president of the Choctaw
Code Talkers Association.

00:00:41.663 --> 00:00:42.825
Around this time, if a Choctaw were caught

00:00:42.825 --> 00:00:44.941
speaking the language, they were liable

00:00:44.941 --> 00:00:47.564
to be sent away to a reeducation program.

00:00:47.564 --> 00:00:50.183
But the captain, he had a different idea.

00:00:50.183 --> 00:00:52.794
- [Nuchi] If I can't
understand what they're saying,

00:00:52.794 --> 00:00:54.535
perhaps the Germans won't understand

00:00:54.535 --> 00:00:55.942
what they're saying.

00:00:55.942 --> 00:00:57.223
- [Narrator] The U.S.
military began transmitting

00:00:57.223 --> 00:00:59.295
technical messages in Choctaw, and,

00:00:59.295 --> 00:01:02.303
unsurprisingly, the Germans
could not break the code.

00:01:02.303 --> 00:01:05.232
Not all military terms had a
direct Choctaw translation.

00:01:05.232 --> 00:01:06.394
- [Nuchi] Machine gun

00:01:06.394 --> 00:01:09.591
(speaking foreign language)

00:01:09.591 --> 00:01:12.159
was little gun shoot fast.

00:01:12.159 --> 00:01:13.292
Ammunition

00:01:13.292 --> 00:01:15.252
(speaking foreign language)

00:01:15.252 --> 00:01:16.950
was arrows.

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:19.682
And gas
(speaking foreign language)

00:01:19.682 --> 00:01:20.682
was bad air.

00:01:22.288 --> 00:01:23.488
- [Narrator] And due at least in part

00:01:23.488 --> 00:01:26.059
to the Choctaw code talkers
and their neologisms,

00:01:26.059 --> 00:01:27.777
the war ended soon thereafter.

00:01:27.777 --> 00:01:28.710
- [Nuchi] When you look at

00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:30.377
how ironic it is for

00:01:31.639 --> 00:01:35.147
the government to say you
can't speak your language,

00:01:35.147 --> 00:01:37.467
but yet they used them

00:01:37.467 --> 00:01:40.078
to help win World War I.

00:01:40.078 --> 00:01:42.238
That's pretty amazing to me.

00:01:42.238 --> 00:01:46.405
(light music)
(cheering)

00:01:48.504 --> 00:01:51.421
(electronic music)

